    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; padding: 10px 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin: 5px 0 0 0;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 9th Grade (CEFR: A2.4)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our Indigenous Heritage</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> Researching Our History</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Summarize articles for classroom discussions. Concept: Explain cultural differences.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can explain cultural differences by using pictures of traditional artifacts and gestures (e.g., points to a handmade Ngäbe-Buglé chácara and explains how it is woven by hand, holds up a Guna Yala mola and describes its vibrant patterns and stories, or shows an Emberá basket and explains how it is crafted from natural fibers like palm leaves).
            Can explain Indigenous cultural differences using visuals and gestures (e.g., shows a picture of traditional clothing and gestures to highlight its colors).
            Can summarize key points from an article about indigenous cultures to help peers understand important traditions and practices during classroom discussions (e.g., creating a mind map about how the Naso Tjër Di celebrate their festival).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 30%; text-align: left; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%; text-align: left; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 20%; text-align: left; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a variety of pictures showcasing different Panamanian Indigenous groups' traditional clothing and artifacts (e.g., a Guna mola, an Emberá basket, a Ngäbe-Buglé chácara). Students are asked to identify them and briefly share one thing they remember or found interesting about them from previous lessons. This activates prior knowledge and reintroduces key vocabulary like 'vibrant patterns,' 'handmade,' and 'traditional clothing.'</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a picture of a Guna mola. "Class, look at this. This is a Guna mola. It has very <b>vibrant patterns</b> and often tells a story. The Guna people <b>preserve</b> their <b>heritage</b> through this beautiful <b>art</b>." The teacher points to and explicitly models the pronunciation of 'vibrant' and 'patterns,' emphasizing the stress. The teacher explains 'preserve heritage' as keeping their culture and traditions alive, and explicitly states, "The Guna people's customs are unique and valuable to our nation," modeling the pragmatic function.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Are molas made by machines or by hand?" (Expected answer: By hand.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do Guna people want to forget their traditions, or keep them alive?" (Expected answer: Keep them alive / preserve them.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short text excerpt (e.g., 150-200 words) or an audio clip (approx. 1-2 minutes) describing a specific cultural aspect of one Panamanian Indigenous group (e.g., the Ngäbe-Buglé's traditional 'balsería' festival or the Emberá's intricate basket-weaving techniques). The material will present information that requires students to summarize and explain, suitable for A2.4 level. Genre: Informative article/cultural blog. Register: Accessible, descriptive. Format: Printed text or audio transcript with accompanying images.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students can understand the main ideas and specific details of informational texts or audio about Panamanian Indigenous cultures and articulate them to others.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is the main tradition or cultural practice described in the text/audio?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> "Match the key term (e.g., 'chácara,' 'balsería,' 'ritual') with its brief explanation from the text."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> "Based on the text/audio, mark these statements as True or False: 'The Ngäbe-Buglé community celebrates 'balsería' only for entertainment.' 'Emberá baskets are crafted from natural fibers like palm leaves.'"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Summarizing information about Indigenous cultures and explaining cultural differences using Present Simple, Past Simple, and Present Perfect, along with descriptive adjectives. Target vocabulary includes: 'Indigenous, cultures, traditions, patterns, diversity, heritage, preservation, vibrant, colorful, handmade, preserve, respect, celebrate, ancestry, community, identity.'</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice (Summarizing Sentence Completion):</b> Students receive short, pre-written paragraphs (3-4 sentences) about different Indigenous groups. Key words/phrases are removed, and students complete the sentences using target vocabulary and correct tense. For example: "The Emberá people _____ their history by making beautiful baskets. They _____ natural fibers for this <b>art</b>." (Expected: preserve, use). "Guna Yala _____ famous for its _____ (mola) with _____ patterns." (Expected: is, traditional clothing, vibrant). This focuses on accurate summarization with core vocabulary.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice (Mediated Explanations with sentence starters):</b> In pairs, students receive two distinct facts about two different Indigenous groups (e.g., "The Guna Yala people are known for their matrilineal society." and "The Naso Tjër Di are led by a Rey/King."). They use provided sentence starters like: "One key difference is that...", "This group ______ their leaders differently because...", "It's important to understand that...", to explain these cultural differences to each other, integrating provided vocabulary and adjectives. The teacher prompts students to focus on stress in 'Indigenous' and 'ancestry.'</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction / Peer Check (Artifact Description and Explanation):</b> Students are given pictures of two different Indigenous artifacts (e.g., a Ngäbe-Buglé mask and an Emberá carving). First, they individually describe each artifact using adjectives like 'colorful,' 'handmade,' 'traditional.' Then, they take turns explaining to their partner how each artifact reflects the group's 'cultural background' or 'beliefs.' Partners provide feedback, checking for clarity and correct use of specific vocabulary ("This artifact <b>preserves</b> their <b>identity</b> because..."). They are encouraged to use gestures while explaining.</li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Task:</b> Students work in groups to prepare a segment for their "Indigenous Heritage Exhibition" (the 21st Century Skills Project). Each group selects one Panamanian Indigenous group and creates a brief 'exhibition panel' (a digital slide or a small poster) focusing on a specific cultural aspect (e.g., traditional clothing, a unique custom, or an important artifact). They must summarize key information researched in previous lessons and prepare to *explain* and *mediate* this information to 'visitors' (their peers or the teacher).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative digital presentation or a poster board 'panel.' Students will verbally present their 'panel' to another group or the class, acting as 'exhibition guides.' They will use visuals (pictures of artifacts/clothing) and gestures (e.g., pointing, illustrating size or weaving motion) to explain the cultural significance, patterns, and traditions. For instance, a group explaining the Guna mola might say: "This is a Guna mola. The <b>vibrant patterns</b> often show <b>traditional</b> animals or geometric designs. Guna women have <b>preserved</b> their <b>heritage</b> by creating these for centuries. A key point to understand is that each pattern has a story, connecting them to their <b>ancestry</b>." (Teacher emphasizes correct stress on 'vibrant', 'traditional', 'ancestry', 'migration', 'identity'). They should include pragmatic expressions like, "Groups like the Emberá have lived here for centuries and maintain traditional ways," when relevant.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme/Scenario:</b> This task directly fulfills the theme 'Researching Our History' and the scenario 'Our Indigenous Heritage' by requiring students to synthesize their research into an informative and explanatory exhibition piece. It explicitly focuses on the mediation skill as students summarize findings, explain cultural nuances, and use various aids to ensure their peers understand important traditions and practices.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on students' ability to summarize, explain, and use target language during the "Indigenous Heritage Exhibition" presentation. A simple peer assessment checklist will also be used where students evaluate another group's presentation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students present their "Indigenous Heritage Exhibition" panel. They must concisely summarize the chosen cultural information and clearly explain at least two important aspects of their chosen Indigenous group's culture (e.g., how an artifact is crafted, the meaning behind a tradition, or a historical detail related to their migration). They are required to use visuals and gestures effectively to aid their explanation, demonstrating their mediation skills.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and accuracy of the summary and explanation of cultural information (Mediation).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate and consistent use of target vocabulary (e.g., 'vibrant,' 'heritage,' 'preserve,' 'customs,' 'ancestry').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct application of Present Simple, Past Simple, and Present Perfect when describing facts, history, and ongoing traditions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective use of visuals and gestures to enhance understanding and explanation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation, with a focus on stress in multisyllabic words like 'Indigenous,' 'identity,' and 'migration.'</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Active collaboration and equitable participation within the group.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Acknowledgement of cultural value and historical importance (pragmatic and sociolinguistic aspects).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new thing I learned about explaining Indigenous cultures effectively today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How well did my group summarize the information and use gestures to explain our chosen cultural aspect?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of summarizing and explaining cultural information to my peers did I find most challenging today, and how can I improve my mediation skills?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can I use what I learned today to better understand and respect other cultures in the future, especially focusing on their customs and traditions?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> Consider what worked well in terms of student engagement with the project, clarity of explanations, and integration of grammar/vocabulary. Did students effectively propose ideas for cultural preservation in their presentations (sociolinguistic competence)? Identify specific areas for adjustment for future lessons or projects, such as providing more scaffolding for summarizing complex texts, further practice with specific pronunciation features (e.g., minimal pairs with /ʌ/ and /uː/), or encouraging more dynamic use of gestures.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concludes the 'Researching Our History' theme and the "Indigenous Heritage Exhibition" project. The skills of summarizing, explaining, and acknowledging cultural value developed in this lesson will be crucial for future themes that involve understanding and communicating about diverse cultures or complex topics.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>